 ## Background
-Originating from the foundational work on neural radiance fields, photometric loss has been the primary supervisory signal for differentiable volume rendering. Early implementations relied heavily on MSE loss due to its simplicity and differentiability [44-radiance-field-optimization.md]. As the field evolved, composite losses like L1+SSIM were introduced to address the blurring effects of MSE [2890-l1-ssim-loss.md]. Techniques such as Visibility-Based Photometric Loss were developed to handle multi-view consistency in sparse-view settings [2917-visibility-based-photometric-loss.md]. More recent methods, such as Adaptive Photometric Loss and Closed-Form Color Estimation, address specific ambiguities in explicit radiance fields and density-color entanglement [9354-adaptive-photometric-loss-with-thresholding.md][10570-closed-form-color-estimation-cf-loss.md].
+The use of photometric losses is foundational in differentiable rendering, originating with the introduction of Neural Radiance Fields (NeRF) which utilized MSE loss for view synthesis [44-radiance-field-optimization.md]. The integration of SSIM was introduced to address the blurriness associated with MSE loss in high-frequency details [2890-l1-ssim-loss.md]. Visibility-based constraints were adapted from multi-view stereo techniques to handle occlusions in differentiable settings [2917-visibility-based-photometric-loss.md]. Closed-form color estimation was developed to resolve the density-color ambiguity in explicit voxel-based representations like Plenoxels and DVGO [10570-closed-form-color-estimation-cf-loss.md]. Temporal consistency losses were introduced for dynamic scene reconstruction to prevent flickering [9372-temporal-consistency-loss.md].
 
